WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.500
A newborn rat muscle fiber is wired to two or more motor neurons.

2
00:00:08.460 --> 00:00:11.740
Every one works. Within weeks, all but one are gone.

3
00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:13.720
What decides which stays?

4
00:00:14.733 --> 00:00:20.213
Each extra input releases acetylcholine and evokes an electrical response in the

5
00:00:20.213 --> 00:00:22.653
fiber. Nothing is wrong with them.

6
00:00:23.473 --> 00:00:24.473
Then they are removed.

7
00:00:25.367 --> 00:00:26.547
How do you count them?

8
00:00:27.347 --> 00:00:31.767
Put an electrode inside one fiber, and slowly turn up the stimulus to its nerve.

9
00:00:32.750 --> 00:00:35.390
Each axon in the nerve has its own threshold.

10
00:00:36.330 --> 00:00:39.010
It fires only once the stimulus passes that level.

11
00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:45.800
Predict. If three axons reach this fiber, what does the response look like as the

12
00:00:45.800 --> 00:00:46.800
stimulus rises?

13
00:00:53.083 --> 00:00:56.903
A staircase. Each threshold crossed adds one step.

14
00:00:57.763 --> 00:00:59.623
Three distinct steps mean three axons.

15
00:01:00.633 --> 00:01:03.653
One axon gives one step, at its own threshold.

16
00:01:04.513 --> 00:01:08.473
One action potential ordinarily reaches all its release sites at once.

17
00:01:09.483 --> 00:01:13.563
In 1970, Redfern did this in newborn rat diaphragm.

18
00:01:14.433 --> 00:01:17.153
Most responses at birth had two to four steps.

19
00:01:17.153 --> 00:01:20.293
By 16 to 18 days, single steps.

20
00:01:21.267 --> 00:01:23.847
So did the extra axons' motor neurons die?

21
00:01:24.817 --> 00:01:26.497
Two measures tell these apart.

22
00:01:27.437 --> 00:01:30.757
Count the motor neurons, and measure one motor unit's share of the twitch.

23
00:01:31.817 --> 00:01:35.637
A motor unit is one motor neuron and all the fibers it supplies.

24
00:01:36.637 --> 00:01:39.757
A twitch is the muscle's brief contraction to one impulse.

25
00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:46.600
Predict. If every fiber gives similar force, what happens to one unit's share if

26
00:01:46.600 --> 00:01:48.880
neurons die? And if they live?

27
00:01:53.283 --> 00:01:58.163
If neurons died, and the survivors still reach every fiber, each keeps its fibers,

28
00:01:58.583 --> 00:01:59.963
so its share stays the same.

29
00:02:00.967 --> 00:02:03.987
If neurons live and trim branches, the count holds.

30
00:02:04.887 --> 00:02:05.927
Each unit's share falls.

31
00:02:06.847 --> 00:02:09.307
This assumes the muscle does not gain many fibers.

32
00:02:10.467 --> 00:02:16.407
In 1976, Brown, Jansen and Van Essen measured both, in rat soleus muscle.

33
00:02:17.467 --> 00:02:19.587
Each unit's share of the twitch fell sharply.

34
00:02:20.447 --> 00:02:23.307
The number of motor neurons did not change significantly.

35
00:02:24.450 --> 00:02:26.870
The neurons lived, and their branches went.

36
00:02:27.850 --> 00:02:30.730
Synapse elimination is branch loss, not neuron death.

37
00:02:31.733 --> 00:02:34.153
Here is the idea we will test through this lesson.

38
00:02:34.153 --> 00:02:37.553
A synapse is not removed just for being quiet.

39
00:02:38.567 --> 00:02:42.727
What puts it at risk is being quieter than its neighbours at the same junction.

40
00:02:47.167 --> 00:02:49.087
The idea says which input goes.

41
00:02:50.367 --> 00:02:51.747
Overall activity sets how fast.

42
00:02:52.927 --> 00:02:55.867
Silence slows it, and some patterns of stimulation speed it.

43
00:02:56.767 --> 00:03:00.907
Quieter seems to mean weaker transmission, as the muscle sees it.

44
00:03:01.427 --> 00:03:03.007
And it is a bias, not a verdict.

45
00:03:04.067 --> 00:03:07.047
So the neurons stay, and each fiber holds a contest.
